  "comparison": {
    "date": "2026-11-07",
    "claim": "Lighter at idle than every other app on a web engine that we measured. Strawberry, a native Qt player, uses about a third of Nuvo's memory.",
    "method": "- **Machine and build.** AMD Ryzen 9 PRO 7945, 30 GB, Fedora, Linux 7.2.11, GNOME on Wayland. Measured on 2026-11-07. Nuvo is the 0.2.10 `linux-unpacked` package. Other programs were running on the machine all day.\n- **Runs and labels.** 5 runs per figure, 6 for Spotify. Every app had its own empty profile and no login. Nuvo: empty profile, Home reached. Liner: sign-in screen. Riff 44.2.0: logged out. Spotify: logged-out client (flatpak). Strawberry 1.2.31: flatpak, empty library. YouTube Music and Apple Music: web tab, logged out, fresh Chrome 156 profile. Each run started a fresh process; the page cache was not dropped.\n- **Idle.** Memory is PSS summed over the whole process tree (Nuvo includes its daemon), read 30 s after the window appeared. CPU is user plus system time of the tree over the next 30 s, in percent of one core.\n- **Cold start.** The definitions differ, so read it as a rough guide. Nuvo, Liner, Riff and the web tabs: first frame with the window visible and text, read over the DevTools protocol. The web-tab figure includes the network. Spotify and Strawberry have no such hook: first mapped window of at least 200 by 200 pixels. Spotify's 435 to 447 ms window is probably a splash, so we give no start figure for it.\n- **Playing.** Nuvo played a cached 90 s tone at volume 0 from a seeded playlist, CPU over seconds 40 to 60. Strawberry played a 240 s tone with its stream volume at 0, measured 30 s after the start. Different players and different moments, both whole-tree. Liner, Spotify, Riff and the web tabs are not measured: playback needs an account, and we did not attempt one.\n- **Load.** One-minute load average under 1.2 at every sample for the other apps. Nuvo's late set had a median of 0.19 and a maximum of 0.29.\n- **Nuvo's spread.** Idle memory moved between two sessions on the same build with no change: 336 MB in an earlier session, 381 MB in the later one, which is the figure above. The same shift of 40 to 45 MB showed in every Nuvo scenario, and we have not found the cause. Every other web-engine app still uses more memory than either figure.\n- **Where Nuvo loses.** Strawberry uses less memory at idle (129 against 381 MB) and less CPU while playing (1.3 against 2.7 %). It is a native Qt player; Nuvo is Electron with a Rust daemon. Strawberry's installed size depends on a shared runtime of about 1.1 GB. Riff's start is bimodal and we have not found why.\n- **Published, not measured by us.** Riff's README claims about 180 MB during playback, with no method given ([source](https://github.com/rootscripts/riff#memory-usage), read 2026-11-07). We measured 422 MB at idle on the AppImage. Other published figures, such as download sizes, are in `.github/readme/published.json`.\n- **Not measured.** Installed size of the web tabs, a start with a dropped page cache, other machines. Nothing here is estimated.",
